        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "Let $n$ be a positive integer. Give $\\mathbb C^n$ the Euclidean topology transported from $\\mathbb R^{2n}$ by the real coordinates $z^i=x^i+i y^i$, where $x^i,y^i\\in\\mathbb R$. Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb C^n$ be open in this topology and contain $0$. Each complex number has its unique real decomposition $a+ib$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$; write $\\overline{a+ib}=a-ib$. A function on $U$ is smooth if all of its partial derivatives of every finite order in the real coordinates $(x^1,y^1,\\ldots,x^n,y^n)$ exist and are continuous. Define the Wirtinger derivatives by $\\partial_i=\\tfrac12(\\partial_{x^i}-i\\partial_{y^i})$ and $\\partial_{\\bar i}=\\tfrac12(\\partial_{x^i}+i\\partial_{y^i})$.\n\nA **Kähler coordinate-patch datum** is such a pair $(U,K)$ with a smooth real-valued function $K:U\\to\\mathbb R$ for which the matrix $g_{i\\bar j}(z,\\bar z):=\\partial_i\\partial_{\\bar j}K(z,\\bar z)$ satisfies $g_{i\\bar j}=\\overline{g_{j\\bar i}}$ and is positive definite: for every $z\\in U$ and every nonzero $v=(v^1,\\ldots,v^n)\\in\\mathbb C^n$, $\\bar v^{\\bar j}g_{i\\bar j}(z,\\bar z)v^i>0$. This smooth Hermitian matrix is the Kähler metric specified by the potential $K$. In natural units $\\hbar=c=1$, mass dimensions are powers of mass; assign $[z^i]=1$, $[K]=2$, and $[g_{i\\bar j}]=0$.",
