o_{R*}(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn},\\alpha})=\\frac{\\mathcal C_{\\alpha,R}}{2i}.$$\n\nIn the fundamental representation, $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}=-2\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}$ therefore gives $\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn}}^\\alpha,\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn},\\alpha})=\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(\\mathcal C^\\alpha\\mathcal C_\\alpha)$, with the same ordered spinor product on both sides. This is a classical representation/component normalization, not a quantum field-rescaling or threshold-matching theorem.\n\nAdopt the connection-normalized microscopic action\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{pure}}=\\int_M d^4x\\left\\{\\frac1{g^2}[\\kappa_H(\\bar\\Phi,e^{2gV_{\\mathrm{ad}}}\\Phi)]_D+\\left(\\left[\\frac1{4g^2}\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn}}^\\alpha,\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn},\\alpha})\\right]_F+\\mathrm{h.c.}\\right)\\right\\}.$$\n\nThe Hermitian-conjugate operation applies only to the chiral F term; the D term is real. Equivalently, the fundamental matrix F integrand is $\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf2}(\\mathcal C^\\alpha\\mathcal C_\\alpha)/(8g^2)$, plus its Hermitian conjugate. Its bosonic gauge density is fixed to $-\\kappa(F_A\\wedge *_\\eta F_A)/(2g^2)$. There is no chiral superpotential, hypermultiplet, hypermultiplet mass, or FI parameter in this ultraviolet datum. The scalar is connection-normalized; its canonical version is $\\phi_{\\mathrm{can}}=\\phi/g$. In the same local Berezin normalization the quadratic microscopic prepotential is $\\mathcal F_{\\mathrm{UV}}(\\Phi)=i\\kappa_{\\mathbb C}(\\Phi,\\Phi)/(2g^2)$, with positive imaginary Hessian $\\kappa/g^2$. The prepotential has mass dimension two. The dimensions are $[x]=-1$, $[\\theta]=-1/2$, $[A]=[\\phi]=1$, $[F_A]=2$, $[\\mathcal W_{\\mathrm{conn}}]=[\\mathcal C]=3/2$, and $[g]=0$.\n\nEight real rigid supercharges, their $\\mathcal N=2$ algebra and invariance of this adopted action are model premises, not a locally proved invariance theorem. Variations are smooth 